A||||13||WisconsinGT||MSD 6A ingnition||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||07-31-2001||01:35 PM||||I am thinking of going this route. Can y'all give me some ideas on where I can install this. I would rather not put it in the trunk area. <P>On a similar note, is it difficult to install? If not how? Do I need other parts? I have the MSD GM original design coil on it now.<P>Thanks!<P>By the way...This is going on a stock 2.8 in my 88GT.||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||07-31-2001||01:40 PM|||| you can mount in under your cruise vacuum canister... just make sure the wires are sheilded from the exhaust manifold heat.<P>follow the instructions..  both white and both pink out of the coil to the black and orange, both the white and both pink out of the distributor to the red and white. the power wires run through the tube in the trunk where your trunk light is. don't try and retain the tach filter you don't need it.. even if you didn't have an MSD box. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Capt Fiero||07-31-2001||04:13 PM||||I just installed a 6A box in my car, it is really really easy, especially if you buy the Adapter kit.  The kit allows you retain all the stock wiring, you just plug the factory connectors into the adapter, wire the adapter to the msd box run a ground and a hot wire and start the car.  I chose to mount mine in the trunk in the recessed part just to the drivers side and a bit under the trunk light.  On a side note, according to all the big manufactures.  Cheaper plugs are better for a high cap ignition system.  The fancy plugs are only worth while to aid in a weak ignition.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||MinnGreenGT||07-31-2001||05:03 PM||||Where did you get the "Adaptor Kit" ...and was it specific to the engine, or car, or???<P>-Rob||||reg||1
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||07-31-2001||05:30 PM||||Just a tip....Don't mount it flat! use Vibration Isolators, Little rubber like feet, this will allow air to flow around the box and cut down on vibration.<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||4
Z||000005||Capt Fiero||07-31-2001||05:43 PM||||It is adapter MSD part number 8876 and is specific to the 2.8 GM dual connector coil, it is made for our style of ignition, it has all factory looking connectors and such, you can buy it from any place that retails MSD, it cost me 45CDN $ about 25 or 30 US$. As for not mounting it flat, that is a very good point that I forgot to mention.  When I mounted mine I used extra long screws and placed a sway bar end link bushing between the trunk wall and the box.  It really does look good. If I can get some pics of it I can post a few to show you how it looks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||johnt671||07-31-2001||06:21 PM||||I mounted mine on the drivers side inner wheel well at a slight angle.  I moved the coil over near the emmissin canister.  this allowed me to mount the unit without any splices excpt the wire to the battery.  The unit and the coil both stay cool to the touch.  I got minne from Jeg's for around 170.00 with the adapter and installed it in a half hr.||||reg||1
Z||000007||grinthock||07-31-2001||07:29 PM||||$27 USD from Summit Racing....  For that adaptor cable, but do you really need it?||||reg||1
Z||000008||California Kid||07-31-2001||08:05 PM||||Caution on "In Trunk" mounting the MSD unit.  This is what I experienced and thought I'd pass it on to you guys.<P>Mounted mine in truck, drivers side, with 3/4 inch stand offs from vertical wall of Carpet, wires went through hole/grommet to engine compartment.  Attachment parts were all metal (no isolator).<P>It operated perfect for two years, then I had a problem with the car, it would only run for about 30 to 60 minutes and shut down. I really mean shut down ! I had to coast to side of road or available parking lot. It would restart like nothing was wrong if you let it just sit for 20 to 45 minutes depending on outside temperature. Took me two weeks to figure out what was happening, which turned out to be "thermal failure" of the MSD Box, which was conducting heat from the trunk sheet metal it was mounted to - through the metal fasteners !!!  Found this out by disconnecting MSD (putting stock parts back in) and it ran just fine for 2 1/2 hours continous, no cutout.<P>I have a replacement MSD, but I'm not going to mount it in this location, even with rubber insulators. Will consider location mentioned in post above near wheelhouse in engine compartment.  Final note: I did notice that the trunk space gets pretty hot, and doesn't appear to be vented at all. So If you have a good deck lid seal, that compartment is hot dead air (no movement, like air in engine compartment).<P>One option to keep it in the trunk location would be to find two locations in the trunk where you could install a 3 inch air inlet and opposite side 3 inch exhaust. Consider water intrusion in the design and use "fart valve" (rubber closure flap) if your exhausting air behind tail lamp (that way when you wash the car you won't get water in the trunk). ||||reg||4
